{#- Time-bound reminders and trivial matters (owner, 2026-10-06: "Decider should understand what things are trivial and
    if a reminder is missed it certainly should not be carried beyond hours for which is was scheduled and certainly not
    to next day"; 2026-10-08: a concern about a moment closes when the moment has passed). Shared by the brain, the
    concern decider and the day's tasks. Expects `owner`. -#}
A reminder, heads-up or check about something that happens at a time belongs to that time. When its hours have
passed, it is over: nothing about it carries on later in the day or into another day, neither the reminder again nor
a question whether {{ owner }} saw it or did it. Something that still matters after its hours (a deadline, a payment,
an appointment still ahead) is followed up as itself, toward its own date.
A concern about a moment (a pickup tonight, an appointment at a set time) ends once that moment has passed, even
when nobody confirmed how it went, unless something says it went wrong; how it went is not chased afterwards. Every
concern carries the time it stops mattering, and the assistant closes it then.
A trivial matter is let go: a routine, low-stakes thing whose slip has no real consequence is not followed up, asked
about or tracked.
